data.content:
## .NET 8 Migration: `EventProcessor`

### Problems in the Original Code

| API | Issue on .NET 8 |
|-----|-----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Unreliable; obsolete in .NET 9 (SYSLIB0046) |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Can't be interrupted by cancellation token |
| Race condition | Multiple enqueues between `WaitOne`/`Reset` loses items |

### Patterns Identified

1. **Cancellable work loop** → `CancellationToken` checked in loop
2. **Blocking call interruption** (`WaitOne`) → `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `token.WaitHandle`

### Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var waitHandles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Blocks until signaled OR cancellation requested
            int index = WaitHandle.WaitAny(waitHandles);

            if (index == 1) // cancellation
                break;

            // Drain all queued items (fixes original race condition)
            while (true)
            {
                string? item;
                lock (_lock)
                {
                    if (_queue.Count == 0)
                        break;
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
                }
                Handle(item);
            }

            _newEvent.Reset();
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel(); // wakes WaitAny immediately
        _thread?.Join(5000);
        // No Thread.Abort fallback — cooperative cancellation only
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

### Key Changes

| Before | After | Why |
|--------|-------|-----|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` | Cooperative cancellation |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Removed — `Cancel()` wakes `WaitAny` | Token's `WaitHandle` unblocks immediately |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny(event, token)` | Cancellation-aware blocking |
| `while (true)` | `while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)` | Clean exit |
| Single dequeue | Drain loop | Fixes lost-item race condition |

### Pitfalls to Watch

1. **"What if `Handle()` hangs?"** — Cooperative cancellation won't interrupt it. Pass `_cts.Token` into `Handle()` and check it at safe points inside long operations.
2. **Don't forget `Dispose`** — `CancellationTokenSource` leaks timers/callbacks if not disposed.
3. **No forcible kill exists** — If `Handle()` truly can't cooperate, move it to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()`.
4. **Reset after drain** — Call `_newEvent.Reset()` *after* draining to avoid missing a signal that arrived during processing.

### Even Simpler: Use `BlockingCollection<T>`

If you don't need the manual threading control, .NET has a purpose-built type:

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable())
            Handle(item);
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _queue.CompleteAdding(); // RunLoop exits naturally
        _thread?.Join(5000);
    }

    public void Dispose() { Stop(); _queue.Dispose(); }
    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

This eliminates all manual synchronization, the `ManualResetEvent`, and the lock entirely. `CompleteAdding()` causes `GetConsumingEnumerable()` to finish gracefully — no abort needed.
